WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:09.673
There are so many strong opinions
about how bad the internet has become.

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:13.635
How it has weaponized mediocrity.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:17.347
Normalized mis- and disinformation.

00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:19.474
But for me,

00:00:19.516 --> 00:00:23.937
the information superhighway
has always been my savior.

00:00:24.729 --> 00:00:25.939
As a little boy,

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:30.485
I was sent from Nigeria to boarding school
in the English countryside.

00:00:31.194 --> 00:00:34.948
It was like "Harry Potter"
without the magic.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.658
(Laughter)

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.578
It was a confusing and troubling time

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:43.665
for an already sensitive little boy
who was far from home.

00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:47.752
I was more like

00:00:47.752 --> 00:00:52.799
an exotic animal in a petting zoo
rather than a student.

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:57.929
And to top it off, I'm dyslexic

00:00:57.971 --> 00:01:02.183
and today what would be described
as neurodiverse,

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:07.063
which for me meant that the classroom
was impossibly slow

00:01:07.063 --> 00:01:12.319
and my brain was not capable
of assimilating how they were teaching me.

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:17.032
So for a big part of my life,

00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:18.992
I was ashamed of my own mind.

00:01:20.076 --> 00:01:24.122
And if I'm being honest,
I was ashamed of myself.

00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:29.669
Luckily, the internet had other ideas.

00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:35.592
Every time I consumed something online,
my brain felt alive.

00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:38.887
It was an awakening of sorts.

00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:42.891
And luckily for me,

00:01:42.891 --> 00:01:48.146
I am the right age to have discovered
the internet in that very, very moment.

00:01:49.105 --> 00:01:51.733
So what did I start doing?

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:54.653
I started realizing how my mind worked.

00:01:55.403 --> 00:02:00.492
How I could assimilate audiovisual
experiences intensely.

00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:06.706
How I could name a song
within the first second or the first note.

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:10.543
Or talk about scenes in films
like I lived them.

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:17.717
And how overwhelmingly powerful
artistic experiences were for me.

00:02:19.344 --> 00:02:22.180
This became my savior.

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:25.642
And I wanted to share it with my friends.

00:02:26.101 --> 00:02:32.732
So pop ballads like
"More Than Words" by Extreme.

00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:38.363
Street poetry like
“Brenda’s Got a Baby” by Tupac.

00:02:38.863 --> 00:02:41.700
Films like "The Last of the Mohicans,"

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.827
"Love Jones,"

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.246
and of course, "Cinema Paradiso."

00:02:47.288 --> 00:02:51.126
These experiences were way more
than entertainment for me.

00:02:51.626 --> 00:02:56.131
I was being raised by these experiences,

00:02:56.131 --> 00:02:59.759
and I needed to share it
with as many people as possible.

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:02.929
So my friends would come
with their hard drives,

00:03:02.929 --> 00:03:08.601
and I would download and share pictures
and poetry and film for them.

00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:15.275
For me, the internet had become
an endless library of the extraordinary.

00:03:15.984 --> 00:03:18.862
And I didn't just have the keys
to this library.

00:03:19.154 --> 00:03:20.405
I lived there.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:26.536
We would talk about Fela Kuti,
Joni Mitchell, Neil Young.

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:29.456
These were my heroes.

00:03:29.456 --> 00:03:33.126
These were the souls
that have found the blueprint

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:37.297
of how to do and win
in this thing called life.

00:03:38.256 --> 00:03:41.259
It also saved my mental health.

00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:46.598
The dancing of Misha Baryshnikov

00:03:46.639 --> 00:03:49.476
was for me, like watching
a perfect sunset.

00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:54.773
The voice of Sarah Vaughan
was my North Star.

00:03:55.482 --> 00:03:59.736
And of course, the words of Pablo Neruda?

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:05.200
Well, that will always be
medicine for my soul.

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:08.203
So,

00:04:08.244 --> 00:04:14.209
how did I get to be
this great observer of content

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.919
and become an artist?

00:04:16.461 --> 00:04:18.254
It was just sharing images.

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:23.468
And one of the images I shared
all the time was this image

00:04:24.177 --> 00:04:26.596
of Coretta Scott King.

00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:31.726
This image was taken by Moneta Sleet Jr.,

00:04:31.768 --> 00:04:35.104
the first Black man to win
a Pulitzer for photography.

00:04:36.105 --> 00:04:41.486
This image almost didn't happen
because unbelievably,

00:04:41.486 --> 00:04:44.531
no Black media was invited

00:04:44.572 --> 00:04:48.910
to photograph the funeral
of Dr. Martin Luther King.

00:04:49.494 --> 00:04:52.914
When Coretta Scott realized, she said,

00:04:52.956 --> 00:04:58.002
"No one's coming into this church
if Moneta Sleet is not allowed."

00:04:58.336 --> 00:05:01.172
And thank the heavens that he was,

00:05:01.214 --> 00:05:05.468
because it was his lens
that captured the grace

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.596
and somehow, on an unimaginable day,

00:05:08.596 --> 00:05:11.683
the strength of Coretta Scott King

00:05:11.683 --> 00:05:17.897
on the funeral of her husband
with her baby girl, Bernice, in her lap.

00:05:18.523 --> 00:05:21.359
It was this image that taught me

00:05:21.359 --> 00:05:27.198
that photography can be way more
than wedding pics and birthday snaps.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.827
It was this image that let me know
that at its best,

00:05:30.869 --> 00:05:36.916
photography can let us know
the work that we need to do.

00:05:38.418 --> 00:05:43.214
(Applause)

00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:48.803
So how did I become
an actual photographer?

00:05:49.304 --> 00:05:51.472
Well, the answer of that is love.

00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:53.141
My wife.

00:05:53.808 --> 00:05:55.018
My wife.

00:05:55.435 --> 00:05:58.479
We fell in love together

00:05:58.521 --> 00:06:02.901
by feeling each other’s
invisible scars like braille.

00:06:03.568 --> 00:06:06.237
She fell in love
with all the parts of myself

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:07.947
that I was ashamed of.

00:06:08.531 --> 00:06:12.702
She looked beyond my anxiety,
my imposter syndrome,

00:06:12.744 --> 00:06:16.915
and she saw a man that maybe
had a point of view himself.

00:06:16.956 --> 00:06:21.252
So she bought me a camera
for my 40th birthday.

00:06:21.878 --> 00:06:23.463
Just five years ago.

00:06:24.297 --> 00:06:26.591
So I have a camera now,

00:06:26.633 --> 00:06:28.217
I don't know what to do with it.

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:30.803
And once again, I go online,

00:06:30.845 --> 00:06:35.934
and I teach myself about ISO
and aperture on YouTube.

00:06:35.975 --> 00:06:41.689
I teach myself about editing
with Adobe Lightroom on YouTube.

00:06:42.315 --> 00:06:44.776
And there's a great beauty
for someone like me

00:06:44.776 --> 00:06:49.822
to be able to fail and fail
in my little office, online,

00:06:49.822 --> 00:06:52.700
watching free tutorials.

00:06:52.742 --> 00:06:55.078
This is a gift of the internet.

00:06:56.162 --> 00:06:59.040
Everything changed
when my daughter was born.

00:07:00.291 --> 00:07:03.670
You see, the thing
about not loving yourself

00:07:03.711 --> 00:07:05.838
is that when something truly great,

00:07:05.838 --> 00:07:10.134
something celestial,
like a child, comes into your life,

00:07:10.176 --> 00:07:12.345
you tell yourself you don't deserve it.

00:07:13.763 --> 00:07:15.932
My daughter was premature,

00:07:15.974 --> 00:07:18.434
and I was terrified to be the custodian

00:07:18.434 --> 00:07:20.395
of something so beautifully precious.

00:07:20.853 --> 00:07:24.107
So I hid behind my camera.

00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:29.862
And as I kept taking pictures of this
little soul and as she got stronger,

00:07:29.904 --> 00:07:35.410
the camera and my daughter
taught me how to receive love

00:07:35.451 --> 00:07:37.787
and taught me to accept

00:07:37.787 --> 00:07:41.833
the immense grace and privilege
of being a father.

00:07:42.291 --> 00:07:45.712
This was the most important moment
in my photographic journey.

00:07:46.629 --> 00:07:50.967
Two years later, in 2020,
George Floyd was killed.

00:07:52.343 --> 00:07:56.681
And all of us saw that
because of the power of social media.

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:00.727
And this time we refused to look away.

00:08:02.186 --> 00:08:05.773
I looked to my wife and I said,
“I have run out of tears.”

00:08:06.691 --> 00:08:08.609
And she said, "Look to your camera."

00:08:09.027 --> 00:08:11.320
And I took my camera
to the streets of London,

00:08:11.362 --> 00:08:15.324
not knowing if I would photograph
five people or 5,000.

00:08:15.908 --> 00:08:17.910
And I was able to observe

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:22.707
one of the greatest civil rights
movements in our lifetime.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:29.088
The global protests
after the death of George Floyd

00:08:29.088 --> 00:08:32.216
is something none of us
would have expected.

00:08:32.258 --> 00:08:33.926
And my lens was there.

00:08:35.845 --> 00:08:40.433
One morning I woke up
and I couldn’t open my phone

00:08:40.433 --> 00:08:43.061
because I had so many notifications.

00:08:44.103 --> 00:08:47.690
The son of Coretta Scott King,

00:08:47.732 --> 00:08:50.318
Martin Luther King III,

00:08:50.318 --> 00:08:55.823
had somehow come across one of my images
and posted it on his Twitter.

00:08:56.783 --> 00:08:59.952
And then the world discovered me.

00:08:59.994 --> 00:09:06.876
(Applause)

00:09:07.168 --> 00:09:11.798
Millions of people saw these images
and now, unbelievably,

00:09:11.798 --> 00:09:14.092
British “Vogue” came calling,

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:16.636
and they commissioned
a then-unknown photographer

00:09:16.677 --> 00:09:22.225
to shoot the September issue
of British "Vogue" 2020.

00:09:22.266 --> 00:09:25.228
And in doing so, I became
the first Black man

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:27.897
to shoot any cover for British "Vogue."

00:09:27.939 --> 00:09:33.528
(Applause)

00:09:33.569 --> 00:09:37.615
It took 104 years to get to my cover.

00:09:38.157 --> 00:09:42.870
Since then, I've had
this extraordinary career

00:09:42.912 --> 00:09:46.582
photographing amazing humans,
amazing moments.

00:09:46.958 --> 00:09:50.294
But beyond all the glitz
and all the glamour,

00:09:50.294 --> 00:09:54.841
it is really important for me

00:09:54.882 --> 00:09:58.094
to recognize the intentionality

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:02.515
and the empathy
of where my lens must look.

00:10:03.641 --> 00:10:06.477
The world is burning right now

00:10:06.519 --> 00:10:10.439
and we cannot pass each other by
like ships in the night.

00:10:10.481 --> 00:10:16.821
So my lens has to look
to where voices need to be lifted.

00:10:18.489 --> 00:10:22.368
I recently have become an ambassador
for Save the Children.

00:10:23.953 --> 00:10:28.166
And I went to Somaliland
to cover the famine,

00:10:28.166 --> 00:10:31.210
the hunger crisis
that is in the Horn of Africa.

00:10:32.503 --> 00:10:36.883
These brave people are suffering
because of climate change,

00:10:36.883 --> 00:10:39.510
something that they add very little to.

00:10:40.261 --> 00:10:43.514
Many of the children
are born into a hellscape

00:10:43.556 --> 00:10:45.641
that is not of their making.

00:10:45.641 --> 00:10:51.814
1.6 million children
are on the verge of acute malnutrition.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:56.402
And I want my images to let you know

00:10:56.444 --> 00:10:58.905
that you cannot say you did not know.

00:10:59.864 --> 00:11:03.618
So I ask you to use whatever levers
of power that you have,

00:11:03.659 --> 00:11:06.329
at dinner parties, on your social media,

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.166
speak to those that can do
something about it

00:11:10.208 --> 00:11:12.168
if you cannot yourself.

00:11:13.836 --> 00:11:15.796
I will finish by saying this.

00:11:17.006 --> 00:11:21.010
Do not be afraid to take
the road less traveled.

00:11:22.261 --> 00:11:25.598
Wear your vulnerability with pride.

00:11:26.057 --> 00:11:27.808
It is what makes us human.

00:11:28.434 --> 00:11:31.479
This age of perceived perfection,

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:32.647
it's over.

00:11:33.147 --> 00:11:37.151
Very few of us are OK, and that's OK.

00:11:37.902 --> 00:11:40.446
And to the parents and teachers

00:11:40.446 --> 00:11:44.492
that are looking after children
with different minds,

00:11:44.533 --> 00:11:47.870
please let them know about the power

00:11:47.912 --> 00:11:51.040
and possibility of their minds.

00:11:51.374 --> 00:11:53.542
Because they may have the answers

00:11:53.542 --> 00:11:58.089
that will allow all of us
to look toward the horizon together.

00:11:58.464 --> 00:11:59.632
Thank you.

00:11:59.674 --> 00:12:04.929
(Applause)

